{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target asserts that an append-only event log requires idempotent writers. However, appending an event to an append-only log is intrinsically non-idempotent (f(f(x)) != f(x)) because it produces two distinct log entries. The property an append-only log actually requires of its writers is safety—retried appends do not violate the system's safety properties (e.g., log integrity or the append-only invariant), even if they produce duplicates. The target's own counterconditions concede that with downstream deduplication, 'the log only needs safe writers.' Furthermore, the target's proposed mechanism relies on 'idempotency keys that the log layer deduplicates,' which means the *log layer* provides idempotency, not the writers. Thus, the writers themselves only need to be safe, directly contradicting the target's main claim.","target":"7db46860ddab8a6597ca6a6e47747dd5e7e88ba63427959f25485af2c868fb59"}]}